{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf57jTVxEwVMtCznBwrg8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Current state\n\n`decompose status rate-limit` → `DECOMPOSE=COMPILED`, **0/4 tasks accepted, 4 unproven capabilities**. The plan is compiled and the topology review is signed (`reviews/delivery-plan-review.json`, disposition `accepted`, reviewer `fixture-controller`), but nothing is materialized — `tasks/` holds only `.plans/`. So everything below is **proposed** structure, not executed or accepted work.\n\n## Why these four are separate tasks\n\nEach leaf owns exactly one seam, one owner, one disjoint write surface, and one independently checkable done condition. The four axes line up one-to-one:\n\n| Task | Seam / owner | Creates (sole write surface) | Done condition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `T-20260802-policy-schema` | SEAM-POLICY-CONTRACT / `platform-contracts` | `src/rate_limit/policy.schema.json`, `tests/test_policy_schema.py` | Schema accepts valid limits/windows, rejects invalid |\n| `T-20260802-effective-policy` | SEAM-POLICY-RESOLUTION / `policy-runtime` | `src/rate_limit/policy.py`, `tests/test_policy_resolution.py` | Resolver returns one stable policy + provenance |\n| `T-20260802-request-101` | SEAM-REQUEST-ENFORCEMENT / `request-runtime` | `src/rate_limit/enforcement.py`, `tests/test_request_101.py` | 1–100 allowed, 101 denied in the same window |\n| `T-20260802-decision-visibility` | SEAM-DECISION-VISIBILITY / `reliability-interface` | `src/rate_limit/decision.py`, `tests/test_decision_visibility.py` | Response and telemetry share reason + decision id |\n\nThree concrete pieces of evidence make the split non-arbitrary rather than cosmetic:\n\n1. **Disjoint write surfaces.** Every unit has `touches_paths: []` and a `creates_paths` set that no other unit names. No two leaves can collide, and each has its own additive `rollback` (\"remove the additive resolver and focused tests\"). Effort is `S` across all four — none is a composition node.\n2. **Independent proof per seam.** Each seam declares its own `independent_proof`, and each leaf's evals hit only its own test file (`test_policy_schema.py`, `test_policy_resolution.py`, `test_request_101.py`, `test_decision_visibility.py`), with a terminal eval covering both behaviors. A leaf can be graded without running another leaf's suite.\n3. **Recorded rejected alternatives.** The seams document what merging would cost: folding the contract into the handler \"would mix contract, resolution, and enforcement concerns\"; resolving policy per-adapter means \"multiple precedence implementations would drift and become unprovable\"; inferring enforcement from metrics fails because \"telemetry observes a decision but cannot authorize or make it.\" That last one is also encoded as an anti-pattern on two separate leaves — it's the authorization boundary between enforcement and visibility, which is why `request-101` and `decision-visibility` are not one task.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`proposed-graph.json` records a strict chain of three `depends_on` edges, matching the steel thread and `ADR-RATE-LIMIT-ORDER` (\"schema, effective policy, request 101 enforcement, then visible reason and decision telemetry\"):\n\n```\npolicy-schema → effective-policy → request-101 → decision-visibility\n```\n\nThe edges follow the seam contracts, not just preference — each consumes the prior seam's product:\n\n- `LEG-POLICY-SCHEMA-VALID` requires nothing, produces *validated policy document*\n- `LEG-EFFECTIVE-POLICY-RESOLVED` requires that document, produces *effective policy decision*\n- `LEG-REQUEST-101-DENIED` requires that decision, produces *allow-or-deny decision*\n- `LEG-DENIAL-REASON-VISIBLE` requires that allow-or-deny decision, produces *visible reason + telemetry event*\n\nThere is no parallelism here: the graph is fully serial, so separation buys reviewable boundaries and independent grading, not concurrency.\n\n## The distinction that matters\n\nSeparate does **not** mean independently startable. Each task is *independently assessable* — its evals prove its own done condition against its own files. But downstream proof still needs its declared dependency satisfied: `test_request_101.py` cannot demonstrate \"no off-by-one drift\" without a real effective-policy resolver, and `test_decision_visibility.py` cannot correlate a reason and decision id without an actual denial to observe. Independence of completion criteria, not independence of inputs.\n\n## Smallest next action\n\n`taskspec batch --plan tasks/.plans/rate-limit/task-plan.json` — this materializes the four **unsealed** leaves. It is not an execution authorization: `gate --stamp` would authorize a specific revision, and `accept` records verified acceptance. All four capabilities remain unproven until then."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1515,"cache_read_input_tokens":35442,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1515},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b50dc51b-759e-4144-823c-3edbcc3e51c7","uuid":"88bd1911-e27d-428f-a03c-100b18dbbbcd","timestamp":"2026-09-15T11:06:25.750Z","request_id":"req_011Cf57jT4AHhAfzwxvzM9yr"}
